WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.223
- Thank you to
brilliant.org for supporting PBS.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.170
- If you're too young
to remember the launch

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.770
of ride sharing apps like Uber and Lyft,

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:13.480
let me tell you, it was magical.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.250
With just a tap of your phone,

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:18.710
a driver would show up in
minutes to take you across town

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:21.940
for a fraction of what a
yellow cab would charge.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.740
Somehow, those wizards of Silicon Valley

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.030
figured out how to
revolutionize the taxi industry.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:30.080
And we all reaped the benefits

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.020
of low prices and immediate service.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:36.600
- Things have changed in the
sharing economy since then.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.820
The average price of a ride share trip

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:43.600
has gone up around 40%
in just the last year.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.710
Airbnb rentals are about
35% more expensive.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:50.340
Many of these companies
point to a pandemic related

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.270
surge in demand and drop in labor supply,

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:56.050
but even food delivery
apps like DoordDash,

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:59.020
which posted strong revenues
throughout the pandemic

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.870
are dramatically raising
their prices as well.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.040
Many experts say that what's driving

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.880
this industry-wide price
hike has less to do

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.080
with the pandemic and more with the fact

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.250
that many of these
companies are finally trying

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.400
to become profitable.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.580
- Wait, you mean more profitable?

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.400
- Nope, I mean profitable, period.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.030
Despite being once valued
at over $80 billion,

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:23.590
Uber has never turned a profit.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:24.893
Far from it in fact.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:38.580
- In 2020, the company lost $6.77 billion.

00:01:38.782 --> 00:01:40.150
And for you blame that on COVID,

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:43.710
keep in mind that's actually
an improvement over 2019

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.340
when they lost a whopping 8.51 billion.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.210
Since its launch in 2009,

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:52.560
Uber has burned through
roughly $20 billion

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.020
of investor capital.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:57.090
And while they are perhaps
the most extreme example,

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.590
they're hardly alone.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:02.710
- Lyft,, DoorDash Grubhub,
Spotify, Snapchat,

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.810
these are just a few of
the many billion dollar

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.780
tech companies, what
insiders call unicorns

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:11.370
that have never made a profit.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.810
Even Airbnb, which was at one time,

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:18.440
the rare unicorn that made money
has started posting losses.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.520
And this was before the pandemic.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.720
Despite becoming a ubiquitous
part of our economic

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.350
and cultural landscape,

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.560
very few of these
companies have figured out

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.000
how to actually make
money doing what they do.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.370
- And yet, investors continue
to pour money into them.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:39.000
In fact, money losing companies
that went public in 2018

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.210
actually did better on the stock market

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:42.930
than ones that made money.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.240
- It doesn't make any sense.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.430
Isn't making profits the
whole point of business?

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.690
- Well, it's not unusual for a startup

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:51.910
to operate at a loss
for some period of time.

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:54.250
After all, you have to
build infrastructure,

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:58.430
so R&amp;D and hire employees
before you can start earning.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.170
What makes the tech sector
unusual is the staggering

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.420
amount of loss investors
are willing to tolerate.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.410
- Amazon has a lot to do with that.

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:08.460
Despite years of losing money,

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.790
and years more of barely breaking even,

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.120
the company was valued higher than firms

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.970
with far bigger profits.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.240
It's all about market dominance.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.480
The idea is to spend gobs of
money to expand aggressively

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.400
without worrying about profitability.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:24.460
So that one day,

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:26.950
you'll be the biggest
player in the industry.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.420
Insiders call this blitzscaling,

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:32.530
and it's pretty popular
on Wall Street these days.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.580
- But 10 years and
billions of dollars later,

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.840
most of these tech companies haven't made

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.130
any tangible progress
towards profitability,

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.940
and it's not clear how much longer

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.070
investors' patients will last.

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:45.903
Think about it.

00:03:45.903 --> 00:03:49.160
Food delivery apps like DoorDash, Grubhub

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.990
literally had a captive customer base

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.750
stuck in their homes
throughout the pandemic

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.900
and still can't figure
out how to make that

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:58.460
a profitable year.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.130
- Some economists think
that the real problem

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:02.550
is that many of these companies

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:06.000
never had a viable business
plan to begin with.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.820
Transportation economist,
Hubert Horan says

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:12.470
that far from revolutionizing
the future of transportation,

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.990
Uber has not solved any of the industry's

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:17.140
longstanding structural problems.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:20.560
And that it has higher costs
than every traditional operator

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.920
in every category other than fuel.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.570
They figured out how to shift costs

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:28.310
like making drivers pay
for their own vehicles,

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:30.930
but that's not really any efficiency.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.840
Having a million
individuals buy and maintain

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.990
their own cars is less
efficient, Hubert argues,

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.730
than buying and servicing a fleet in bulk.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:42.270
The company will eventually
have to make up the difference

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:45.760
in the form of higher wages if
they want to attract drivers.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:47.540
- So then were they ever able

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:49.630
to offer such low prices?

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:53.230
Because their investors were
essentially subsidizing them.

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:56.090
New York Times technology
reporter, Kevin Bruise

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:58.810
compares the industry's pricing to a store

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:02.020
that sells $1 bills for 75 cents.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:05.120
Sure, word of mouth will be fantastic.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.120
But if you ever wanna make money,

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.680
you'll have to raise
your prices to say 125.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.120
And if you think your customers

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:15.130
will stick around for that, good luck.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:18.160
- This is not to say that
there aren't some tech firms

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:19.970
that are genuinely innovating

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:22.440
or that Uber might not someday figure out

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.550
how to make taxis more efficient.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:27.850
But what's clear is that many
players in the sharing economy

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:32.020
only got where they are through
artificially low prices.

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:34.850
There's a mystical aura around tech

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:36.550
that makes it seem plausible

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:40.790
that a ride from the
airport could only cost $5.

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:43.980
But in reality, the only
innovation was a willingness

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.330
to lose money on every transaction.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:50.150
Even Amazon isn't really
performing miracles.

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:53.400
The majority of their
relatively small profit margin

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:57.740
comes from their web hosting
service, not the retail sector.

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:58.573
- So what?

00:05:58.573 --> 00:06:00.960
You might think, if a
bunch of rich investors

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.633
wanna subsidize my convenient lifestyle.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.700
I admit we certainly
partook in what journalist,

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:11.980
Kara Swisher called assisted
living for millennials.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:14.060
Her words, not mine.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:17.020
- However, while very
few of these tech brands

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:19.250
have managed to achieve the dominance

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.510
of say Facebook or Amazon,

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.070
they have acquired enough market power

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:25.970
to put some serious pressure

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:28.140
on traditional small businesses.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:30.940
Restaurants that felt
compelled to play ball

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:34.240
with DoorDash and Grubhub
are now being squeezed

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.970
by commissions as high as 30%.

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:39.240
And the taxi industry, which for years

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.050
was carefully regulated
to ensure profitability

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:44.700
and broad socioeconomic access

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.770
has been decimated by Uber's low prices,

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:51.440
which no profitable business
could ever hope to match.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.470
Chillingly, a recent
internal document from Uber

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:59.340
identified public transportation
as their next competitor.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.350
- Today, as these companies
start to tighten their belts,

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:05.780
prices are going up and
maybe that's a good thing.

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:09.620
Maybe a ride from the airport
should never have cost $5

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.020
in the first place.

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:12.457
As Kevin Roose, put it,

00:07:12.457 --> 00:07:14.470
"Getting someone to clean your house,

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:18.090
do your laundry or deliver
your dinner should be a luxury

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:20.890
if there's no exploitation involved."

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:23.560
As it strives to narrow its losses,

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:26.550
Uber has been steadily
lowering its labor costs,

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:30.200
down to below minimum wage
according to some studies.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:32.130
And now surprise, surprise,

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:34.504
they're having trouble
finding enough drivers.

00:07:34.504 --> 00:07:38.440
Perhaps their self-driving
division could pick up the slack

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:41.340
except they sold that off in late 2020

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.810
for a gain of 1.6 billion.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:45.470
And it still wasn't enough to offset

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:47.440
their operating losses that quarter.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:50.420
- Now, we're not saying you
should dump your Uber stock

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:51.970
if you happen to have it,

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:53.150
all this may sound bad,

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:56.680
but Wall Street doesn't seem
to be overly bothered by it.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:59.330
Maybe Uber will someday become the Amazon

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:01.590
of transportation that they hope to be.

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:02.890
But even if they don't,

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:05.510
we should be weary about
what these companies

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:07.120
are trying to accomplish

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.740
because the end game of blitzscaling

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:12.220
sounds less like modern innovation

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:15.060
and more like old fashioned monopoly.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.070
And the only point of having a monopoly

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:20.810
is to be able to squeeze
your workers and customers.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:23.520
- Ideally, the market
should reward companies

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.630
that truly innovate and make
industries more efficient

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:29.840
and productive, and some
are genuinely doing that.

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:32.850
- But we shouldn't let the
mystique of tech distract us

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:35.420
from the fact that some
of the biggest players

00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:39.220
have just been buying
their way into the game.

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:41.020
And that's our two cents.
- And that's our two cents

